answer_text:
This behavior seems very fishy to me.  Importing the same module in different ways will still have your class (Base) refering to the same class object.  It seems unlikely that you could get into this situation via importing.



However, one workaround is to use super:



[CODE]
class Base(object):
    def __init__(self):
            print "Base!"

class Inherited(Base):
    def __init__(self):
            super(Inherited,self).__init__()
            print "Inherited"

class Base(object):
    def __init__(self):
            print "Base!"

Inherited()

[/CODE]



super ensures that you're actually calling the base-class's __init__ rather than some other class which happened to take the place of the base-class in the current module's namespace.

